\chapter{Local dependencies, Git locks, and offline builds}
\label{ch:64}
\index{Local dependencies, Git locks, and offline builds}
\index{Projects!lock files}

Local source dependencies are declared with their project information. Remote Git branch or tag intent must be resolved to an immutable commit through a separate locking step. The fetch command acquires the selected Git source over the documented HTTPS profile. The normal build consumes resolved inputs and does not acquire them implicitly. A frozen build uses the lock and previously fetched artifacts and rejects inconsistent state.

\section{Rehearse a clean acquisition}
Generate the lock in the intended repository, inspect the resolved revision, and acquire the dependency into the documented cache. Then run a frozen build with network access removed. Change the dependency declaration locally to demonstrate that the old lock no longer matches. Capture the exact lock and provider in a bug report. A source dependency lock pins that input but does not pin the host compiler's sysroot or every optional native tool. Keep C and SDK evidence alongside the lock for native builds.

\section{Which revision prints the answer?}
Let a remote library's \OnxCodeTerm{stable} branch point at commit A,
whose function returns seven. Declare the branch, run the project lock
command with the remote available, then build a client that prints the
function's result. The lock entry records both the selector (branch,
stable) and the full resolved commit. The object cache is keyed by the
dependency name and commit; a lock entry by itself does not contain
the library's source bytes.

Advance the remote branch to B, changing the answer to nine. On the
original machine a frozen client build still prints seven: it validates
the old entry and reads A. On another machine with the same manifest and
lock but no cached objects, the same request fails. It cannot obtain seven
from the lockfile alone, and nine would violate the recorded resolution.
An ordinary online lock refresh can select B; inspect the commit change
before distributing the refreshed lock and inputs together.

Repeat the thought experiment with a local Git store. A dirty edit to its
working copy is invisible to a build that reads pinned commit blobs. With
a \OnxCodeTerm{local} dependency the same edit to a declared module is
visible and changes its fingerprint. To interpret a report of ``the same
library gave a different answer,'' ask which of these input models was
used. A filesystem path naming a Git repository does not turn a local
source declaration into a commit pin. The lock records the dependency
kind as well as its source, so switching the declaration from one model to
another is visible even if today's module bytes happen to agree.

